Hi Murray,

Thanks for the clarifications. For point #3, specifying my covariate in newdata easily resolved the problem, so that seems all good! Thanks.

For point #1, I tried to fix the pID parameter to different values. In most of the cases, the model provides an estimated pID equal to 1 (even though I fixed it at lower values, such as 0.01 for example), which results in identical density estimates and loglik. Whenever the estimated pID obtained is lower than 1, the resulting density estimates are different and the loglik (and AIC) becomes a lot larger.

